      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I got a bit carried away with the goblin analogy, didn't I? Let me try to refocus on the physics.\n\nNewton's third law is indeed crucial in understanding the lift generated by an airfoil, like a wing. When air flows over the curved surface of the wing, it creates a pressure difference between the upper and lower surfaces. This pressure difference is a result of the action-reaction principle, where the forward motion of the plane (the \"action\") is matched by an equal and opposite reaction from the air.\n\nAs the air flows over the wing, it accelerates downward, creating a region of lower pressure above the wing. This downward acceleration is a result of the air's reaction to the wing's forward motion. Conversely, the air flowing along the lower surface of the wing accelerates upward, creating a region of higher pressure below the wing. This pressure difference creates the lift force that counteracts the weight of the plane.\n\nTo illustrate this, imagine a goblin named Grip, who's an expert at manipulating air currents. Grip uses his goblin cunning to create a swirling motion behind the wing, which accelerates the air downward, creating the pressure difference that generates lift. Meanwhile, the air flowing along the front of the",
